Effects of the Stuxnet Cyberattack on the Natanz Uranium Enrichment Facility: An evaluation of the 
first known cyberattack in history and an analysis of possible Iranian security hardening procedures at 
Natanz. 
By Lawrence Fox, MA, MBA 
February 24, 2020 
Introduction 
Stuxnet is the first known cyberattack in history against critical infrastructure (Langner 2013, 1).  
This attack resulted in the decommissioning of approximately 1000 Iranian centrifuges at the Pilot Fuel 
Enrichment Plant in Natanz, thereby effectuating a ten percent reduction in functioning centrifuges  
(Albright, Brannan, and Walrond 2010).  Unauthorized US government leaks revealed that Operation 
OLYMPIC GAMES was the code name for a joint United States, Israel, Netherlands, Germany, France, 
and United Kingdom cyberattack designed to frustrate Iranian nuclear ambitions (Sanger 2012).  The 
operation is still classified, however, the Stuxnet virus eventually found its way into civilian systems 
and was extensively analyzed in the academic domain, leading to substantial public interest.   
Human intelligence aspects of the operation were leaked into the media and culminated in the US 
government's prosecution of US General James Cartwright; this offense was pardoned by President 
Obama prior to sentencing, to maintain plausible US deniability for operation OLYMPIC GAMES 
(Fingas 2017). Despite the moderate success of the attack, it is important to note that Iran keeps at least 
50% of its centrifuges as spares (Langner 2013, 1).  Removal of the damaged centrifuges was directly  
confirmed by International Atomic Energy Agency inspectors and continued operations in the wake of 
the Stuxnet attack were problematic.  During this time, the Iranian government continued to 
manufacture low enriched uranium at Natanz at reduced efficiency levels (Albright 2010). 
Thesis 
The Stuxnet attack against Iran was a modestly successful covert intelligence operation, as it 
damaged approximately 1000 centrifuges, eroded Iranian confidence in their ability to locally 
manufacture and master nuclear technology, resulted in the dismissal of several scientists, embarrassed 
the Iranian government on the world stage, placated Israel and established a political norm for the use 
of cyberweapons, thereby managing uncertainty facing future decision-makers use of cyberweapons. 
Literature review 
The Stuxnet worm is particularly interesting, as it is the first known malware to damage industrial 
control systems under real-world conditions.  This literature review is focused on exploring three main 
concepts.  Preventing the introduction of malware through security policies and procedures, detecting 
the effects of malware, and reducing the vulnerability to Stuxnet type attacks in the future.  The most 
important discovery from reading the publicly available literature on the Natanz uranium facility 
cyberattack, was how preventable the attack was.  Ralph Langner's, “To kill a centrifuge” is the most 
complete analysis of the computer vulnerabilities used in the Stuxnet malware.  Langner's work is 
particularly important, because it reveals how the computer network actually controls the centrifuge 
array.  Langner's executive summary states, “post-Stuxnet mitigation strategies like emphasizing the 
use of air gaps, anti-virus, and security patches are all indications of a failure to understand how the 
attack actually worked”.  As a U.S. Navy contract network administrator with 10 years of experience in 
classified and restricted environments, to include sensitive compartmentalized information facilities, I 
concur with Langner's assessment of the international academic response to the Stuxnet attack.  Stuxnet 
is more of a physical security policy problem, than a computer security problem, as it relied on physical 
access to the Natanz computers (Langner 2013, 11). 
None of the academic or anti-virus corporate assessments of the computer malware extensively 
addressed the military and scientific nature of the facility the attackers were targeting.  Only Langner's 
assessment actually discussed the centrifuges in depth and his  assessments are the most insightful in 
the public domain.  It is surprising that none of the  postmortem reports in the public domain discuss 
the idea of installing independent, redundant and tamper-resistant safety hardware, for the sole purpose 
of detecting and stopping a hazardous situation.  The Iranian centrifuge developers understood that 
vibration sensors were important and added them  to their system since, both their P1 and P2 Zippe
type related centrifuge designs cycle through resonant frequencies during normal operation (Langner 
2013, 1).  Prolonged operation at resonant frequencies results in catastrophic failure (Langner 2013, 1).  
Unfortunately for the Iranians, these vibration sensors were connected to a compromised system and 
their warnings were suppressed by Stuxnet (Langner 2013, 9).  Creating independent, redundant and 
tamper-resistant safety mechanisms wherever possible, is an important tool for mitigating the severity 
of future cyberattacks. 
A series of assessments by the Institute of Science and International Security gives a detailed 
assessment of Iranian centrifuge configurations and provides a skilled analysis of raw International 
Atomic Energy Agency report data.  ISIS reporting concentrates on the technical engineering 
challenges involved in uranium enrichment and Iranian progress in developing low enriched uranium 
(Albright, Brannan and Walrond 2010).  These technical aspects must be understood to appreciate the 
intermittent failure style attack sequence used by Stuxnet. 
A 2015 news report by Reuters titled, “Exclusive: U.S. Tried Stuxnet-Style Campaign against North 
Korea but Failed - Sources” reports in some detail about a failed attempt to sabotage the North Korean 
nuclear program with Stuxnet (Menn 2015).  The report indicates that the attack failed because there 
was no way to get the malware onto the “core machines that ran  Pyongyang’s nuclear weapons 
program” (Menn 2015).  This source confirms striking similarities between the Iranian and North 
Korean program.  The Reuters report confirms the obvious; physical control over an air gapped system 
is an effective method of preventing both data exfiltration and sabotage (Rist 2006). 
“Stuxnet under the Microscope” by Matrosov, Rodionov, Harley and Malcho, provides an in depth 
assessment of the dates and times of the malware's development.  A detailed explanation to the 
installation of the malware via USB is provided.  The presence of a malicious .LNK file icon being 
displayed in certain versions of Windows, is enough to execute malicious code, the user does not need 
to actually run the program (Matrosov, et al. 2020).  The ESET anti-virus company provided a superior 
analysis of the malware, with intermediate level IT professionals as the target audience. 
The Symantec “W32.Stuxnet dossier” provides an excellent overview of the worm and offers some 
small points not found elsewhere (Falliere, Murchu and Chien 2010).  No resource tends to have every 
feature or point covered, a reading of all the various reviews is necessary to obtain the most complete 
picture of the Stuxnet malware.  Western anti-virus companies tend to avoid addressing the NSA's 
involvement in the Stuxnet attack.  Kaspersky's paper, “Equation Group: questions and answers” is the 
most complete document in the public domain highlighting NSA involvement (Kaspersky 2015).  
Kaspersky does not actually use the term NSA, but rather calls them the “Equation Group” in their 
papers.  The authors are quite obviously referring to the NSA.  This paper gives specifics on IP 
addresses, malware command and control server domain names, specific methods used by NSA to 
compromise website ads, countries that are off limits to hacking attacks, artifacts such as project code 
names that were revealed during forensic analysis of Stuxnet and other interesting operational details 
(Kaspersky 2015).  Eastern sources disclose a wealth of information on NSA tactics, techniques and 
procedures. 
David Kushner's article, “The Real Story of Stuxnet” details how several other pieces of malware 
have been associated with the NSA after analysts observed that they share portions of code (Kushner 
2013). As a result, Duqu, Flame, and Gauss have all been associated with the NSA (Kushner 2013).  To 
remove any doubt, highly classified NSA documents leaked by Edward Snowden and published in full 
by The Intercept, confirm that Flame malware is of NSA origin (The Intercept 2015).  In the literature, 
a common theme is that forensic analysis of malware frequently leads to clues, even from even state 
actors with the resources and knowledge to avoid such artifacts in their compiled code.  In the case of 
Stuxnet, several references were quite intentional (Kushner 2013).   
Most importantly, the Stuxnet worm is the product of an intelligence agency and was designed 
to accomplish an intelligence mission (Zetter 2014).  Kim Zetter, a major author on Stuxnet related 
news, wrote in a Yahoo News article, “Dutch intelligence agency AIVD provided critical data that 
helped the U.S. developers target their code to the systems at Natanz, according to four intelligence 
sources” (Zetter and Modderkolk 2019).  The article gives critical details on operation OLYMPIC 
GAMES, a joint effort established to frustrate Iranian nuclear activities between the US, Israel, the 
Netherlands, Germany, France and the United Kingdom.  Through the Dutch intelligence agency AVID, 
a mole setup two Iranian front companies to apply to work on contracts at Natanz (Zetter and 
Modderkolk 2019).  Months after Stuxnet’s discovery, the Iranians arrested and likely executed several 
workers at Natanz believing they assisted in delivering the malware onto systems at the plant (Zetter 
and Modderkolk 2019). Two intelligence sources who spoke to Yahoo News stated that this was true 
however, they did not comment if the Dutch mole was caught, presumably to preserve a sense of 
unease in the Iranian intelligence services (Zetter and Modderkolk 2019). 
The aforementioned leaked NSA documents confirm an active state of cyber warfare, with a 
trilateral group consisting of the US, UK and Israel, allied against Iran (The Intercept 2015).  The 
document mentions, “. . . Iran's discovery of network exploitation tools on their networks in 2012 and 
2013;”  prove that the US, UK and Israel have continued  post-Stuxnet offensive cyber operations 
against Iran (The Intercept 2015).  The document also confirms Iranian cyberattacks against Saudi 
Arabian and US computers (The Intercept 2015). 
Evaluation criteria 
Considering that the objective of the Stuxnet cyberattack was to damage the Iranian uranium 
enrichment centrifuges at the pilot fuel enrichment plant at Natanz, operation OLYMPIC GAMES 
succeeded in that mission.  The International Atomic Energy Agency confirmed that  approximately 
1000 centrifuges were removed from Natanz.  This attack was directly authorized by President Barack 
Obama, in furtherance of the US government's long standing nuclear non-proliferation policies (Sanger 
2012).  The US, nor any of its partners, have suffered any political  blow back from this operation, 
despite the escape of the Stuxnet computer worm into the public domain. 
When determining if Operation OLYMPIC GAMES was a success, we can answer a simple,  “yes 
or no” question to conclude that the mission was accomplished.  Covert actions, like war, cannot be 
considered successes or failures through simple accounting.  An effective cost benefit analysis of this 
operation considers the impact on overall uranium enrichment, the length of time  Iran was set back in 
Uranium production, effects on both Iranian and US international reputations, opportunity costs to the 
US by making certain computer vulnerabilities public, reducing the potential international outcry 
surrounding the use of  cyberweapons, by first using them against an unpopular state actor and lastly, 
diplomatic considerations between the US and Israel. 
Cyberattacks do not rise to acts of war 
Cyberattacks are without question, the cutting edge weapon of the future.  They offer policymakers 
the choice of a non-kinetic attack, one that can still incapacitate enemy infrastructure, usually without 
direct loss of life.  This expands policymakers’ range of options when using force to achieve a policy 
goal.  General Hayden former director of NSA and CIA observed, "I would not call it an act of war" 
referring to the Stuxnet attack against Iran in a CBS news interview (Krofk 2012).  "Look, given my 
own background Steve, I've got this whole universe of things between peace and war that are called 
covert actions ... The Iranians have not quite responded to it as if it were an act of war. It's in that space 
between the two" (Kroft 2012).  An important aspect of using the Stuxnet worm was establishing that 
cyberattacks resulting in the damage of equipment, even at sensitive nuclear facilities, do not rise to 
acts of war.  The Iranians have implicitly agreed to this, in that they did not take any overt military 
action against any of the states involved in operation OLYMPIC GAMES post-attack. 
Contrast this outcome with the US drone assassination of Major General Qasem Soleimani, head of 
Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, on January 2, 2020.  The Iranian government  responded to 
the assassination with a missile strike against nearby US forces, based out of the Al Asad and Irbil  
airbases, belonging to the Iraqi government (BBC News 2020).  The Iranians fired Fateh-313 and 
Qiam-1 surface to surface missiles (BBC News 2020), each having a throw-weight of ~500Kg and 
750Kg respectively (Missile Defense Project 2020).  Sixteen missiles were fired in a strike that 
ultimately resulted in no US fatalities, but left 64 US soldiers’ brain-damaged, according to a Pentagon 
statement (Schogol 2020).  Clearly, the Iranians are willing and able to use lethal force against US 
military personnel.  Through Iranian inaction, we can conclude they do not perceive cyberattacks on 
critical infrastructure, not resulting in loss of life, to be an act of war.  In light of these facts, the Stuxnet 
attack reduced political uncertainty surrounding the use of cyberweapons and created an international 
norm that these weapons do not constitute acts of war. 
Beyond the use of cyberweapons in covert actions, cyberweapons can be used to complement 
kinetic operations.  If a military commander is given the choice between the use of lethal weapons 
system and a cyber-attack, to attack a power grid in a heavily populated civilian area, it is likely the 
commander would pick the option that would preclude civilian injuries or fatalities, no matter how 
accurate the weapon system employed.  As an additional inducement to prefer cyberattacks against 
critical infrastructure where possible, after the facility has been captured by US  forces, an event that 
can occur over a time span of mere hours, it is likely to be physically undamaged and can be brought 
back into operation under US control.  This accelerates the inevitable post conflict rebuilding effort, 
while reducing costs and perhaps most importantly, cyberweapons allow the US to reinforce its war 
effort with captured enemy infrastructure and resources. 
Iran is learning cyberwarfare from our attacks 
Perhaps more concerning is the future enemy use of cyberweapons against the US and other western 
state actors with substantial military resources.  NSA's own documents state that, “Iran . . . has 
demonstrated a clear ability to learn from the capabilities and actions of others" (The Intercept 2015).  
By repeatedly attacking Iran with high-quality malware, Iranian programmers have learned from these 
attacks and have improved their own capabilities.  Kim Zetter, a writer for Wired magazine, documents 
a post-Stuxnet attack against Iran called Wiper.  The malware was designed to erase files and crash 
computers owned by the Iranian Oil Ministry & the National Iranian Oil Company in April 2012 
(Zetter 2015).  After this attack, Iran attacked Saudi Aramco computers with Shamoon, an attack 
resulting in total data loss from ~30,000 computers (Zetter 2015).  The corporate disruption was severe 
and Aramco temporarily stopped selling fuel to local gas tanker trucks for 17 days as it was unable to 
collect payment (Pagliery 2015).  Aramco ultimately started giving away fuel to local vendors  free of 
charge, simply to keep gasoline available to Saudi consumers (Pagliery 2015).  Kaspersky researchers 
have confirmed that Shamoon is a less advanced, copycat of the Wiper malware (Kaspersky 2017). 
Clearly, cyberattacks allow highly skilled individuals, non-state groups and particularly militarily 
weak state actors to cause massive disruption inexpensively and potentially unattributively to a more 
powerful adversary.  Otto von Bismark is quoted as, “Fools learn from experience. I prefer to learn 
from the experience of others”.  It should be of concern to everyone in the intelligence community that 
the United States has not learned from the Iranian security blunders exploited in the Stuxnet attack.  
The Department of Defense's own assessments confirm that “cyber capabilities of other nations exceed 
U.S. ability to defend systems” (O' Hanlon  2017).  In furtherance of this goal, the academic 
community should make every effort to publicly analyze the available information on the subject and 
formulate an actionable strategy to minimize risk to US and allied infrastructure operators.  In light of 
these facts, it is apparent that the US faces opportunity costs every time it uses innovative malware 
against an adversary.  Previously unknown security vulnerabilities, once used operationally, become 
known and therefore unavailable for future operational use.  Additionally, the malware used in an 
attack will be analyzed by the victim and frequently used in future copycat attacks. 
Did Stuxnet meaningfully delay the Iranian LEU program? 
The best information in the public domain concerning Iranian enrichment progress is obtained from 
IAEA reports.  Unfortunately, the Iranians added centrifuges during the IAEA reporting period making 
the actual impact of the attack unclear (Albright, Brannan, and Walrond 2010).  An IAEA report dated 
November 16, 2010 revealed a shutdown of all Natanz centrifuges (Albright, Brannan, and Walrond 
2010).  Approximately a week later, Iran informed the IAEA that 28 cascades were operating.  “On 
November 23, Ali Akbar Salehi, the head of Iran’s Atomic Energy Organization, confirmed to IRNA 
that the virus had indeed reached Iran: “One year and several months ago, Westerners sent a virus to 
[our] country’s nuclear sites” (Albright, Brannan, and Walrond 2010).  While a one-week total 
shutdown of enrichment at Natanz is clearly a success and a psychological operations victory, the 
ultimate impact on Iranian enrichment is fleeting.  Any long-term damage effects caused by Stuxnet, 
such as shortening the operational life of the affected centrifuges is unknowable.  Considering the 
addition of new and improved Iranian centrifuges, these effects are unlikely to have any significant 
strategic impact on Iran's enrichment program. 
Developing cyberattack resistant enrichment infrastructure 
Forearmed with the knowledge that supercritical designs, like the P1 and P2 Zippe-type centrifuges 
(Broad 2004) must pass through resonant speeds, the Iranians may choose to integrate resonance 
countermeasures into their designs.  Marvin Miller, Senior Scientist Emeritus at both the MIT Center 
for International Studies, is quoted that, “ A low-tech solution to the problem of flexural vibrations with 
long rotors is to break up the length of the rotor with short, thin flexible joints located so as to take up 
the energy of the vibrations at the resonant speeds. While this method is still used in relatively short 
Aluminum and Maraging steel centrifuges, longer, faster CFRC [Carbon Fiber Resin Composite] 
machines instead use special drives that can accelerate the rotor rapidly through the resonances before 
the amplitude of the vibrations grows sufficiently to damage the machine (Miller 2004).  By integrating 
flexible joints into CFRC centrifuges, reliance on electronically controlled drives to make a quick 
transition through resonant frequencies is less important, lessening the opportunity for sabotage or 
cyberattack (Albright and Stricker 2010).  Iran's latest experimental centrifuge, the IR-8 implements a 
vibration dampening rotor design (Albright and Stricker 2017).  ISIS reports that “. . . [In the IR-8 
design] the bellows connect the rotor tubes together and allow the entire rotor assembly to be flexible 
and escape destructive resonant vibrations”.  ISIS report reveals that the IR-8 centrifuge design has four 
rotor tubes and 3 bellows (Albright and Stricker 2010).  
ISIS considered the likely effect of a Stuxnet style resonance attack on Iran's new carbon fiber rotor 
design and concluded that resonance effects would be less destructive against a P2 centrifuge with 
carbon fiber rotors, as they are more able to withstand high-speed stress (Albright and Stricker 2010).  
When Iran eventually transitions to its IR-8 centrifuge design, their risk to Stuxnet type resonance 
attacks will be greatly decreased.  An additional layer of protection, the integration of an optical 
tachometer connected to an independent, redundant, and tamper-resistant controller could stop the 
centrifuge automatically if the acceleration or operational characteristics deviate from a predefined 
profile.  Such an arrangement would mitigate the impact of a hardware damaging attack, by reducing 
the attack to a mere denial of service. 
Commercially available sensors such as the Laser Tach LT2 tachometer sensor, for example, allow 
for measurements up to 100,000 RPM  from distances as far as 20 in (51 cm) and unlike magnetic 
tachometers, laser sensors do not require the rotating equipment to be a ferrous material, as only a 
retro-reflective target needs to be attached to the shaft (LT2 Online Catalog Listing 2017).  Laser-based 
optical tachometers could provide the Iranian operators at Natanz great flexibility in integrating an 
external sensor into any centrifuge.  IR-4 Iranian centrifuges are only partially made of maraging steel 
and make extensive use of carbon fiber, an easier to acquire but still embargoed material (Albright and 
Waldrond 2011).  Iran has completely eliminated maraging steel from its post-IR-4 designs and has 
transitioned to aluminum, making sensors compatible with non-ferrous materials a relevant design 
consideration moving forward.  Lastly, integrating a laser sensor need not be particularly expensive.  
The Neiko 20713A handheld Laser Tachometer detects speeds up to 99,999 RPM and is available on 
Amazon for $22.39 USD at the time of writing (Amazon 2020).  If Iran desires a proactive approach 
toward detecting abnormal centrifuge equipment activity, an optical tachometer connected to an 
independent, redundant and tamper-resistant controller is an excellent option. 
In the Stuxnet case, a USB drive loaded with malware was used to introduce Stuxnet into the  local 
network controlling the centrifuges.  Since the network is not connected to the Internet, the only 
methods of introducing malware is via removable media or through the network cable.  Iranian 
operators can secure their workstations by physically hardening the computers used by nuclear 
technicians.  Considering the high-security nature of nuclear facilities, it would not be unreasonable to 
expend additional resources to increase physical security.  The first step would be to place the existing 
PC's in hardened, locked, and alarmed enclosures to eliminate unauthorized physical access.  Second, 
USB devices would be prohibited due to security flaws, only PS/2 type keyboards and mice would be 
authorized and printers would use only parallel ports (Anthony 2014).  By eliminating any physical 
access to wiring that could communicate data to the computer system, the workstation cannot become 
infected while sealed.  Only the keyboard and mouse and parallel port cables will be exposed when the 
PC security container is locked.  The unauthorized infiltration or exfiltration of data would become 
impossible. 
Third, network wiring should be converted to 100% fiber optic cable, due to its inability to leak 
information via electromagnetic radiation and its immunity to electromagnetic interference (Campbell 
1999).  Common metallic wiring is subject to electromagnetic induction taps.  The US government 
used this vulnerability this to tap Soviet undersea cables in the 1970's in operation IVY BELLS 
(Campbell 1999).  Furthermore, fiber optic cable is physically difficult to tap and results in measurable 
optical signal loss (Ghosh 2013).  Security consultant Bernard Everett observes, “One method [to tap 
fiber optic cable] is to bend the cable and extract enough light to sniff out the data” (Ghosh 2013).  
These fiber optic cable runs can be further protected by covering them with a hose pressurized with 
nitrogen gas.  If the cable is penetrated the pressure will be released, alerting armed guards to 
immediately investigate the situation.  This approach was used unsuccessfully by the Soviets to protect 
underground communication cable runs.  In operation GOLD, US intelligence successfully defeated 
this protection through the use of a pressurized box (Wallace and Melton 2008, 531).  In this 
application, the fiber optic cable is much more difficult and time consuming to tap physically, than 
copper wiring and there is limited excess fiber optic cable to bend. 
If the wiring is installed in a high visibility location, regularly patrolled by armed guards and visible 
to Natanz employees, it is unlikely that any intelligence asset would have enough time to successfully 
tap the pressurized hose, tap the fiber optic cable, connect a computer to the network, hack a vulnerable 
system and conceal any physical damage to the pressurized hose, while simultaneously avoiding 
detection from patrolling armed guards and ordinary employees.  To mitigate against internal threats, a 
two person integrity (TPI) rule should be implemented to open a workstation's security enclosure for 
maintenance or any other special purpose.  To implement TPI in this situation, two high security locks 
are installed on the computer security container, two authorized persons must be present while the 
container is unsecured and lastly, one person can never have both keys to gain lone access to the 
container (US Navy 1997). 
Stuxnet embarrassed the Iranians 
A report by the RAND corporation mentions, “... Iran's reaction to having been deeply embarrassed 
by Stuxnet, a hack that not only exposed Iran's inadequate approach to cybersecurity but also its 
fecklessness in running industrial enterprises” (Libicki 2015).  It is likely that embarrassment is the 
largest effect of the Stuxnet attack.  Ralph Langner is quoted as, “When comparing the Pakistani and 
the Iranian uranium enrichment programs, one cannot fail to notice a major performance difference. 
Pakistan basically managed to go from zero to successful LEU production within just two years in 
times of a shaky economy, without the latest in digital control technology. The same effort took Iran 
over ten years, despite the jump-start by the Khan network and abundant money from sales of crude oil. 
If Iran’s engineers didn’t look incompetent before, they certainly did during Operation Olympic 
Games” (Langner 2013, 16).  Ultimately, the damage done was similar to what would happen if 
Stuxnet simply overloaded all the centrifuges at once, simply  destroying everything at one time 
(Langner 2013, 16).  Stuxnet was conceived as low-yield weapon designed to reduce the lifespan of 
Iran’s P1 centrifuges, while making the engineering team look incompetent. 
Considered in its entirety, operation OLYMPIC GAMES was an effective psychological operation, 
demoralizing the Iranian government and its technicians.  As previously mentioned, the long-term 
effects on the affected Iranian centrifuges is unknown however, the affected centrifuges are but a small 
percentage of Iran's active program and only had a minor negative impact on Iran's strategic reserve of 
centrifuges.  The real insult was that the Americans literally laughed at the Iranians on the world stage.  
President Obama’s chief strategist for counter WMD, Gary Samore, declined to answer a Stuxnet 
question, while saying with a smile, “I’m glad to hear they are having troubles with their centrifuge 
machines, and the U.S. and its allies are doing everything we can to make it more complicated” (Broad, 
Markoff and Sanger 2011). 
Placating Israel 
Israeli civilians are so terrified of a Nuclear-armed Iran, that 51% of Israelis favor an immediate 
strike on Iran as a first resort, according to a 2009 poll conducted by Tel Aviv University (Ynetnews  
2009).  In 2012, Israel asked the US to assist Israel with advanced “bunker-buster” weapons that would 
improve its ability to effectively strike underground Iranian nuclear sites (Lubell 2012).  Israel has long 
been militarily targeting Iranian nuclear interests (Lubell 2012).  Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin 
Netanyahu indicated that Israel considered military strikes against Iran (Raviv2014).  Reporter Dan 
Raviv admitted that sources close to the Mossad were pressured by the Obama Administration to stop 
assassinating Iranian nuclear scientists inside Iran (Raviv 2014).   
In a shocking admission from Meir Dagan, then director of the Mossad, the Israeli's had a five-point 
approach to disrupting Iranian nuclear ambitions. Israel's action plan called for “heavy international 
diplomatic pressure, economic sanctions, support to Iranian minorities and opposition groups to help 
them topple the regime, the disruption of consignments of equipment and raw materials for the nuclear 
program and, finally, covert ops, including the sabotage of installations and targeted killings of key 
figures in the program” (Bergman 2018).  Covert operations between the US and Israel included: the 
Stuxnet attack, joint sabotage operations to achieve breakdowns in black market equipment destined for 
Iran, intercepting shipments of equipment destined for Iran, President Obama's announcement outing 
the hidden enrichment plant at Qom, and the increasing of economic sanctions against Iran (Bergman 
2018). 
The targeted killing of scientists was strictly a Mossad operation, never discussed with the 
Americans because Executive Order 12333 prevented Americans from involvement in assassination 
(Bergman 2018).  Bermgan reveals that “The Iranians also became paranoid about the possibility that 
all the equipment and materials they’d acquired on the black market for their nuclear project—for very 
large sums of money—were infected, and they examined and reexamined each item over and over.  
These efforts greatly slowed the progress of the nuclear project as a whole” (Bergman 2018).  
Ultimately, the success or failure of Stuxnet must be viewed as a part of a multifaceted strategy to 
dissuade Iran from continuing its nuclear pursuits.  Currently, Iran is far from a viable nuclear weapon 
however, its program preserves the ability to develop one in the future, once its domestic programs are 
more robust (Bergman 2018).  This position is reinforced by Director of CIA, General Michael 
Hayden, who told President Obama, “There isn’t an electron or a neutron at Natanz that’s ever going to 
show up in a nuclear weapon. What they’re building at Natanz is knowledge. What they’re building at 
Natanz is confidence, and then they will take that knowledge and that confidence and they’ll go 
somewhere else and enrich uranium. That knowledge, Mr. President, is stored in the brains of the 
scientists.”  Stuxnet was an important demonstration of solidarity with its close ally Israel.  It is vital to 
continued US / Israel relations, that the Israeli's believe, that the US takes Iranian nuclear issues 
seriously, even if the US currently refuses to destroy the Natanz facility with military force. 
The Stuxnet attack was a success, even if carried out for this reason and no other.  Without the US 
“doing something”, Israel may have exercised a military option unilaterally, potentially leading to 
regional instability.  Meir Dagan claimed that Prime Minister Netanyahu had directed Dagan to order 
preparations for an attack, “... I have decided to place the IDF and you at O plus 30”.  Dagan further 
comments, “O plus 30” was short for “30 days from an operation,” meaning that Netanyahu was 
preparing for an attack on Iran without going through proper channels (Bergman 2018).  With 51% 
public support for a first strike against Iran, this account seems highly plausible (Ynetnews 2009). 
Conclusions 
An extensive examination of the available information on the subject of Stuxnet, confirms that 
Stuxnet was a modestly successful attack with important political ramifications. Stuxnet did result in 
the decommissioning of approximately 1000 Iranian centrifuges and was therefore a minor victory.  
More importantly, the Iranians were publicly embarrassed on the world stage.  The joint Stuxnet 
operation allowed the US to reassure Israel, that the US was a reliable military ally, despite its 
reluctance to use military force to destroy Natanz.  This achieved the important US policy goal of 
maintaining good relations with an important ally, in a key geographic location.  Furthermore, by 
assisting Israel with a cyberattack on Natanz, instead of a kinetic attack, the US preserved stability in 
the middle east.  Stuxnet was also the first time a state actor used a cyber weapon against critical 
infrastructure. The attack was clearly successful and it demonstrated offensive US cyberwarfare 
capabilities, while announcing to the world that the US is willing and able to fight in the cyber domain.  
Additionally, the international community will certainly take note that, no state actor targeted by a 
covert cyberattack retaliated with military force, thereby creating political norms for the use of 
cyberweapons against another state actor.  In light of the establishment of norms concerning the use of 
cyberweapons against major companies and infrastructure, US politicians are likely to feel more certain 
about the non-kinetic response of other nations targeted by cyberweapons.  Considering the extensive 
offensive capabilities of NSA, this may result in the US ultimately employing more cyberweapons 
operationally. 
Stuxnet can be perceived as a failure, if the only measure of effectiveness is a major disruption of 
low enriched uranium production or a major loss of centrifuges.  Stuxnet was not able to achieve these 
goals and was not designed for this purpose.  In fact, long term fatigue in some of the low capacity 
cascades are ultimately unimportant, since Iran possesses more reserve centrifuges than they plan to 
use operationally and the newly developed Iranian IR-8 centrifuge has 16 times the uranium processing 
capacity of Iran's unreliable, first-generation P1 systems (Albright 2014).  Even a year's production 
setback of a P1 centrifuge would be strategically irrelevant in light of new Iranian centrifuge 
technology. 
Another negative aspect of the Stuxnet attack was the inevitable discovery of a high-quality 
malware sample to forensically examine and ultimately emulate.  The Iranians made a workable copy 
of the US Wiper malware used to attack the Iranian Oil Ministry & the National Iranian Oil Company.  
The Iranian copycat malware dubbed Shamoon worked adequately when deployed operationally,  
resulting in the paralysis of Aramco and causing a 17-day local fuel shortage in Saudi Arabia 
(Kaspersky 2017).  While Iranian cyber capabilities will inevitably increase, the NSA's resources dwarf 
those of Iran; budget documents leaked by Edward Snowden reveal that the NSA's budget for 2013 was 
10.8 Billion USD (Andrews and Lindeman 2013).  While the Iranians may develop a competent cyber 
capability, the intelligence community should not expect Iran's cyber capabilities to exceed NSA 
capabilities in the foreseeable future based on economic disparities alone.  The Stockholm International 
Peace Research Institute (SIPRI) reported that Iran’s total military expenditure was $13.2 Billion USD 
in 2018.  The NSA's 2013 budget alone is almost 11 Billion USD, and that's just one US spy agency 
(Tian et al. 2018). 
Lastly, an interesting neutral outcome of the Stuxnet attack is that academics and analysts, now have 
a real world example of a cyberattack against critical infrastructure to analyze.  It is surprising that the 
literature on Stuxnet does not widely address the idea of implementing independent, redundant and  
tamper-resistant safety hardware, designed to prevent dangerous conditions in industrial control 
systems in the event of a cyberattack.  Nor does it adequately address the need for physical security 
surrounding computers and their networks, when defending against state level adversaries.  
Unfortunately, it is unlikely that sensitive industries in the US will take serious steps to secure  
infrastructure vulnerabilities until crippling attacks occur domestically, or until operators of critical 
infrastructure are compelled by legislation to do so. 
